{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-350802",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{1-2t})\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{6})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ -2{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -12{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{1-2t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12{t}^{5})\\,\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3/2}} \\\\ 4{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -60{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
